e, bounded by the Balkan Mountains, the Black Sea, the Aegean and the Sea of Marmara, and now divided between…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/thracia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thracia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Thracia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Thracia was a province of the Roman Empire created by the emperor Claudius in 46 CE after the murder of the client king Rhoemetalces III, covering much of present-day…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak-tomb"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kazanlak-tomb.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak is a beehive-shaped brick tomb near Kazanlak in central Bulgaria, discovered in 1944 and dated to around 300 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/thracian-tomb-of-sveshtari"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thracian-tomb-of-sveshtari.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Thracian Tomb of Sveshtari</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Thracian Tomb of Sveshtari is a royal tomb of the early 3rd century BCE near the village of Sveshtari in north-eastern Bulgaria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/thracians"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thracians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Thracians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Thracians were an ancient Indo-European people of south-eastern Europe, living in the lands of modern Bulgaria, northern Greece, European Turkey and parts of Romania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/thrasybulus-of-miletus"><span class="enc-name">Thrasybulus of Miletus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Thrasybulus was the tyrant of Miletus in the late 7th and early 6th centuries BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/thrasymachus"><span class="enc-name">Thrasymachus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Thrasymachus of Chalcedon was a Greek sophist and teacher of rhetoric active in Athens in the late 5th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/three-bishoprics.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Three Bishoprics</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Three Bishoprics were the cities and ecclesiastical principalities of Metz, Toul and Verdun in Lorraine.</span></a></li></ul>
